.FIRE WEATHER...
Issued at 452 AM PDT Tue Aug 4 2026

Elevated fire weather conditions continue through the remainder of
the week due to dry daytime conditions across the interior with
near critical conditions returning late week into the weekend.
Temperatures remain around 10-15 degrees above normal across the
interior with the exception of Wednesday and Thursday where
temperatures are more seasonal. High temperatures largely remain
in the upper 80s to 90s with the warmest locations reaching the
low 100s. Daytime humidity retention remains poor across the
interior (20-30%) with humidity values dropping into the 10s
across the interior Central Coast, East Bay Hills/Valleys, and the
higher elevations. Portions of the interior Central Coast may see
daytime humidity values drop into the single digits Tuesday, then
again towards the end of the week into the weekend. Overnight RH
recovery remains poor at locations above 2,000 feet with values
between 25 to 40% expected with some favored locations struggling
to reach even 25%. This will be particularly true across the
Mayacamas, highest peaks of the East Bay Hills, the Diablo Range,
the Santa Cruz Mountains, the Santa Lucia and Gabilan Ranges.
Nocturnal drying (rapid decrease in RH through the pre- dawn
hours) may also transpire which will increase the burn period or
time in which fire behavior may remain active. Energy Release
Component (ERC) values have climbed above the 97th percentile
within the Interior Central Coast and Diablo-Santa Cruz (Diablo
Mountains and Santa Cruz Mountains) zones with the Mid Coast to
Mendocino Zone (North Bay) to reach the 97th percentile this week.
Large fires become more frequent after ERC values cross the 90th
percentile with extreme burning conditions expected with ERC
values above the 97th percentile. Diurnally breezy onshore winds
are expected along the coast, mountain gaps and passes, and along
the ridgelines each afternoon/evening. Given the elevated fire
weather concerns and very dry fuels, individual across the highest
risk areas (interior North and East Bays, the Diablo Range, the
Santa Lucia Range, and interior Central Coast) are advised to
avoid activities that may spark a fire. This includes
campfires/barbecues and making sure chains are secured to avoid
sparks if towing anything. Fire crews are urged to exercise extra
caution when executing any prescribed burns, especially where
fuels, topography, and weather align.

